{
  "case_id": "2004-04-6432",
  "document_hash": "54d12418d20dc1b6b09319c2afcd4d4397b7e5203125fe2b1b5bb1f5c893a3c6",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Supreme Court",
      "case_id": "2004-04-6432",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "54d12418d20dc1b6b09319c2afcd4d4397b7e5203125fe2b1b5bb1f5c893a3c6",
      "evidence_id": "ev-54d12418d20d-0000-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 1,
        "start": 1
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 11:05 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner (Paul M. Rashkind)",
      "case_id": "2004-04-6432",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "54d12418d20dc1b6b09319c2afcd4d4397b7e5203125fe2b1b5bb1f5c893a3c6",
      "evidence_id": "ev-54d12418d20d-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "We confront today the Eleventh Circuit's categorical and jurisdictional prohibition of rule 60(b) in habeas corpus cases absent fraud. That is a rule that has been rejected by nearly all of the other circuits and, in fact, has been rejected by the United States in its amicus brief filed in this case."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner (Paul M. Rashkind)",
      "case_id": "2004-04-6432",
      "confidence": 0.85,
      "document_hash": "54d12418d20dc1b6b09319c2afcd4d4397b7e5203125fe2b1b5bb1f5c893a3c6",
      "evidence_id": "ev-54d12418d20d-0000-0003",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "There have only been, since AEDPA was passed, 28 successful motions that we can quantify, that are published in any way."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice O'Connor",
      "case_id": "2004-04-6432",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "54d12418d20dc1b6b09319c2afcd4d4397b7e5203125fe2b1b5bb1f5c893a3c6",
      "evidence_id": "ev-54d12418d20d-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 19,
        "start": 19
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE O'CONNOR: But why isn't that always the case if it's time-barred?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Rashkind",
      "case_id": "2004-04-6432",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "54d12418d20dc1b6b09319c2afcd4d4397b7e5203125fe2b1b5bb1f5c893a3c6",
      "evidence_id": "ev-54d12418d20d-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 19,
        "start": 19
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "One of the nice things about rule 60(b) is it really is a disciplined approach to a court examining its own mistakes. It isn't a wide-open door in any respect. It is a disciplined approach. There are six specific grounds, and even though the sixth one looks like it's wide-open, it certainly isn't under the jurisprudence of the Court."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Rashkind",
      "case_id": "2004-04-6432",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "54d12418d20dc1b6b09319c2afcd4d4397b7e5203125fe2b1b5bb1f5c893a3c6",
      "evidence_id": "ev-54d12418d20d-0001-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "One reads AEDPA and one thing is very clear. They -- Congress intended for a person who has exhausted claims, not procedurally defaulted them in State court, and has filed a timely petition, that person under 2254 is entitled to have the claim entertained."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice O'Connor",
      "case_id": "2004-04-6432",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "54d12418d20dc1b6b09319c2afcd4d4397b7e5203125fe2b1b5bb1f5c893a3c6",
      "evidence_id": "ev-54d12418d20d-0001-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE O'CONNOR: Well, now, the Federal Government has a different proposed rule than yours. Are you going to comment on their proposal?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2004-04-6432",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "54d12418d20dc1b6b09319c2afcd4d4397b7e5203125fe2b1b5bb1f5c893a3c6",
      "evidence_id": "ev-54d12418d20d-0001-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 22,
        "start": 22
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: But the -- the Government, by the way, seems a broader rule than yours. The only thing it rules out is new legal claims or new evidence. I don't see anything in your -- tell me if I'm wrong, but I don't see anywhere where you say we should be able to bring a 60(b) motion based on new legal claims or new evidence."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Rashkind",
      "case_id": "2004-04-6432",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "54d12418d20dc1b6b09319c2afcd4d4397b7e5203125fe2b1b5bb1f5c893a3c6",
      "evidence_id": "ev-54d12418d20d-0001-0008",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I think ultimately we come down to three issues that support the position that we're taking. Chief Judge Edmonson made note of this in his concurring and dissenting opinion."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2004-04-6432",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "54d12418d20dc1b6b09319c2afcd4d4397b7e5203125fe2b1b5bb1f5c893a3c6",
      "evidence_id": "ev-54d12418d20d-0001-0009",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 33,
        "start": 33
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: So is that also true if his own witness has committed the fraud?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2004-04-6432",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "54d12418d20dc1b6b09319c2afcd4d4397b7e5203125fe2b1b5bb1f5c893a3c6",
      "evidence_id": "ev-54d12418d20d-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 36,
        "start": 36
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Do we have that anyplace at -- in the papers before us, the first -- the first dismissal in the Florida -- in the Florida trial court?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Stevens",
      "case_id": "2004-04-6432",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "54d12418d20dc1b6b09319c2afcd4d4397b7e5203125fe2b1b5bb1f5c893a3c6",
      "evidence_id": "ev-54d12418d20d-0002-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "But is it correct that they address the same subject matter? Isn't 60(b) directed at the integrity of the habeas proceeding, whereas AEDPA is directed at the integrity of the original conviction?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2004-04-6432",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "54d12418d20dc1b6b09319c2afcd4d4397b7e5203125fe2b1b5bb1f5c893a3c6",
      "evidence_id": "ev-54d12418d20d-0002-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Didn't Congress rule -- rule out 60(b) in -- in death cases?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Millett",
      "case_id": "2004-04-6432",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "54d12418d20dc1b6b09319c2afcd4d4397b7e5203125fe2b1b5bb1f5c893a3c6",
      "evidence_id": "ev-54d12418d20d-0002-0003",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 43,
        "start": 43
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Ms. MILLETT: Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the Court: Justice Breyer, let me assure you that our position is, if not as strict, only marginally less strict than the State of Florida's."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Millett",
      "case_id": "2004-04-6432",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "54d12418d20dc1b6b09319c2afcd4d4397b7e5203125fe2b1b5bb1f5c893a3c6",
      "evidence_id": "ev-54d12418d20d-0002-0004",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 44,
        "start": 44
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. MILLETT: A rule 60(b) motion that seeks to set aside a final judgment denying Federal habeas relief on the grounds that it was incorrectly decided is a second or successive petition under AEDPA. It can proceed only under AEDPA's terms, which change not only the standards for a second decision, but the decision-maker, the gatekeeper."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Millett",
      "case_id": "2004-04-6432",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "54d12418d20dc1b6b09319c2afcd4d4397b7e5203125fe2b1b5bb1f5c893a3c6",
      "evidence_id": "ev-54d12418d20d-0002-0006",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 46
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. MILLETT: I am saying a lot more than that, and that is, first of all, because the vast majority of things that are covered by 60(b) do not qualify as tantamount to fraud or a biased judge."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Millett",
      "case_id": "2004-04-6432",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "54d12418d20dc1b6b09319c2afcd4d4397b7e5203125fe2b1b5bb1f5c893a3c6",
      "evidence_id": "ev-54d12418d20d-0002-0008",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 48,
        "start": 48
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. MILLETT: If he does it within 10 days under rule --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2004-04-6432",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "54d12418d20dc1b6b09319c2afcd4d4397b7e5203125fe2b1b5bb1f5c893a3c6",
      "evidence_id": "ev-54d12418d20d-0003-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 53,
        "start": 53
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The first State habeas was dismissed because it was not notarized. That's the sole basis for its dismissal: it was not notarized."
    }
  ],
  "schema_version": "1.0.0"
}
